odels themselves were we're actually  incompatible with each other  like I could not it's impossible to  translate one model into the other right  that they have logically distinct  assumptions it's not possible for both  of these models to be right okay it's  possible that they're both wrong but  they can't both be right  it's possible that they could both be  right under different domains right but  they're actually talking about the same  domain so there's this sort of really  serious problem that we are we have  these theories that can't both be right  that claim to be talking about the same  thing and so I was surprised not just  that this was sort of still going on but  that apparently nobody else had even  noticed this problem right that you just  have people who follow a grin avetis  paper and you have people who followed  Victor and Danny at all and they just  talk past each other so why is this able  to persist so I'm going to talk a little  bit about that but I think you know why  is it matter well I think you know if we  think that doing social science is  motivated by the desire to inform social  practice make the world a better place  whatever you know all the reasons that  we got into doing social science in the  first place or at least that I got into  doing social science in the first place  we should be troubled by this  observation so you know one reason that  I bring this up is that there's been a  lot of excitement recently about  computational social science and how  we've been sort of struggling for a  hundred years now to get really good  data on individual individual behavior  and and interactions or think networks  at scale over time right so this is an  enormous kind of data collection problem  and social scientists have struggled  with this problem for many years and  then sort of almost all of a sudden it  seems we have all this data that is  delivering almost for free exactly or  approximately the type of data that  we've been that we'v