aining and you have a  theoretical view of the world you have a  theoretical framework and very often you  have a methodological framework as well  that goes along with that and you take  that toolbox and you go and you apply it  to problem X problem Y problem Z  meanwhile your colleague over in a  different department has a different  theoretical framework in a different  toolbox and they are also addressing the  same problems XY and Z but the output  that you produce is very often  incompatible or very difficult to  compare and again if you go and read a  sociology paper about collective action  and you read an economics paper about  collective action  you know the titles look the same but  after that it's it's difficult to see  how these people are actually talking  about the same thing okay and it's  because they have these very different  theoretical frameworks that they bring  today  and you might think well that's fine you  know that's actually good to have a  diversity of approaches to these complex  problems and I would agree with that but  it really depends on how those  approaches are integrated with each  other and what kind whether or not they  have any kind of useful interaction with  each other and I would say that the way  we have organized ourselves is that they  do not that we publish in separate  journals and they just think these  things go out into the world and they  just sit there and they talk past each  other so how would we change this and  it's it's sort of the subtle seeming  change but I think it is fundamental  that if you start just with a problem we  just wake up one day and say I'm  interested in this problem I don't know  whether it's called sociology I don't  know whether it's called economics I  don't know whether its corporate I don't  know what it's called right I just know  that it's a problem in the world and  problems in the world don't particularly  respect disciplinary boundaries they  just are whatever they are and let me 