ult thing and you  know helping people out of poverty is  not nearly as difficult so why can we do  the first thing and we can't do the  second thing and I think we probably  know the answer to that that they're  just different sorts of problems that  these engineering problems have exactly  the properties that I mentioned on the  previous slide that they're very well  defined you know when you've you've  solved them and many problems in society  we can't even agree really on what the  problem is and as soon as we sort of get  into an argument about it it becomes  clear that everybody has all sorts of  different agendas and outcome metrics so  so that's certainly true and I think  that that kind of puts a lot of possible  problems off the table  right I think it's also true that that  there are instances where social  scientists have contributed very useful  valuable insights to to real problems  and a colleague of mine at NYU when I  was talking about this said well you  know what about the Earned Income Tax  Credit right this is something that  sociologists came up with and it has  demonstrably reduced poverty and I  thought wow I don't know is that a good  example it's kind of a good example it's  like a it's a really good thing but it's  not clear to me that the world would not  have figured out the Earned Income Tax  Credit without the help of sociologists  right and I think it's certainly not  clear - you know it's not clear in the  sense that you're not going to figure  out how to build the driverless car on  your own and you know this is possibly  an unfair comparison and I you know I  actually have spent a lot of time  writing and thinking about how unfair it  is that people think everything in  social science is obvious even when it's  not but  it would be nice if we could find  something that was clearly not obvious  right that we could say this is a  problem you agree it's a problem you  agree that you don't know how to solve  it we solved it okay and he